  "summary_interpretation": "Matched 646 E-PRTR facilities to 22,583 procurement contracts across 23 EU countries. Overall: single-bidder contracts match to facilities with +65.3% higher CO2 emissions than multi-bidder contracts (statistically significant, p=0.0000). Raw RDD at \u20ac139k: above-threshold contracts go to higher-emitting facilities (\u03c4=0.1305, p=0.0112, significant). Note: positive \u03c4 means above-threshold (more competition required) \u2192 higher CO2, reflecting that larger contracts go to larger industrial suppliers. After country+sector+year FE residualization, the threshold effect becomes small and not significant (\u03c4=0.0677, p=0.4546), indicating the raw RDD effect is driven by sector/country composition, not a causal threshold mechanism. 5/8 E-PRTR sectors show statistically significant SB-emission premium (2 positive: SB\u2192higher CO2, 3 negative: SB\u2192lower CO2). CAUSAL CHAIN EVIDENCE: STRONG. Facility-level E-PRTR data supports the hypothesis that reduced competition channels procurement toward higher-emitting facilities. The strongest evidence comes from the within-sector analysis (Energy: +131.6%, Mineral: +36.0%), confirming the technical channel operates within industrial sectors. The RDD threshold effect attenuates after sector/country FE, suggesting the E-PRTR carbon link operates through allocative composition rather than a sharp threshold mechanism."
}